*(
    title: Genetic Technologies
    description: Deliberately changing a genome, from selective breeding to cutting and pasting DNA directly.
    tags: recombinant DNA, transgenic, cloning, gene therapy, module 6, biology, stub
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Humans have been altering genomes since the first domesticated crop, by choosing which organisms breed. What changed recently is the resolution: whole genomes selected blindly, then individual genes moved on purpose.

    Written out here: artificial insemination and artificial pollination, cloning, recombinant DNA and the transgenic organisms it produces, and gene therapy — with the differences in precision and in risk between selecting an organism and editing one.

    What those techniques are actually used for, and what their spread does to biodiversity, is *Biotechnology*.

    internote(ref: biotechnology.chalk)
}
